     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg per day. That is double the usual maximum for adults, which is 3,200 mg per day under medical supervision. The usual single dose is 400\u2013800 mg. A dose this high raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **You aren't a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from a licensed clinician with prescribing authority for this patient. That clinician can verify the indication and the patient's full picture.\n\nIf the prescriber meant something different, they can confirm the intended dose. For example, 800 mg three times daily is 2,400 mg per day, and 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours is also common. They can then enter the order themselves, or ask me to enter it with the corrected dose. If the dose really is intended, it needs to go through pharmacy review and be documented by the prescriber."
      }
     ],
